1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
Most cities put up signs to tell you where things are.

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:04,774
Las Vegas did it backwards.

3
00:00:04,774 --> 00:00:08,204
It put up the signs first, and the city grew in behind them.

4
00:00:08,954 --> 00:00:15,126
In 1932, the biggest sign in town was a neon beer stein over a downtown gambling hall.

5
00:00:15,876 --> 00:00:25,125
Ninety-one years later, the biggest sign in town was the building: a sphere wrapped in 580,000 square feet of LEDs.

6
00:00:25,875 --> 00:00:36,669
In between: a talking cowboy, a sign so tall it had an elevator inside it, a design nobody bothered to copyright, and a graveyard where the city's most famous lights go when they die.

7
00:00:37,419 --> 00:00:40,883
This is the story of how Las Vegas built itself out of light.

8
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:45,866
Las Vegas started as a railroad stop.

9
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:54,676
In May of 1905, the railroad auctioned off lots in the middle of the Mojave, and a dusty little town took shape around Fremont Street.

10
00:00:55,426 --> 00:00:58,005
For twenty-five years, not much happened.

11
00:00:58,005 --> 00:01:01,654
Then, in 1931, two things changed everything, weeks apart.

12
00:01:01,654 --> 00:01:03,918
Nevada legalized wide-open gambling.

13
00:01:03,918 --> 00:01:08,321
And the federal government started building Boulder Dam down the road.

14
00:01:08,321 --> 00:01:14,926
That meant thousands of workers, and eventually more electricity than a small desert town could ever use.

15
00:01:15,676 --> 00:01:18,182
A sign painter from Ogden, Utah noticed.

16
00:01:18,182 --> 00:01:24,571
Thomas Young had opened his own sign shop in 1920 with three hundred dollars borrowed from his father.

17
00:01:24,571 --> 00:01:30,459
Passing through Las Vegas in 1931, he figured the new gambling halls were going to need signs.

18
00:01:30,459 --> 00:01:31,023
Big ones.

19
00:01:31,773 --> 00:01:36,819
His Young Electric Sign Company, YESCO, started working in Las Vegas in 1932.

20
00:01:36,819 --> 00:01:41,013
One of its first clients was the Boulder Club on Fremont Street.

21
00:01:41,013 --> 00:01:44,290
Its sign showed a stein of beer, pouring in light.

22
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:49,472
And Young made a business decision that mattered as much as any design.

23
00:01:49,472 --> 00:01:52,469
Most casino owners were running on a shoestring.

24
00:01:52,469 --> 00:01:55,528
So instead of selling them signs, he leased them.

25
00:01:55,528 --> 00:01:56,901
The club got the sign.

26
00:01:56,901 --> 00:01:58,212
YESCO kept the steel.

27
00:02:04,762 --> 00:02:08,456
After World War Two, Fremont Street turned into an arms race.

28
00:02:08,456 --> 00:02:11,847
The Pioneer Club, the Las Vegas Club, the Golden Nugget.

29
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:16,267
Every owner wanted something bigger and brighter than the club next door.

30
00:02:16,267 --> 00:02:18,750
And the sign makers were happy to oblige.

31
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:22,155
The street got a nickname: Glitter Gulch.

32
00:02:22,905 --> 00:02:25,857
Its most famous resident showed up in 1951.

33
00:02:25,857 --> 00:02:34,369
Years earlier, the Chamber of Commerce had hired a New York ad agency to give the city a mascot: a friendly, winking cowboy.

34
00:02:34,369 --> 00:02:38,626
The Pioneer Club had YESCO build him in neon, forty feet tall.

35
00:02:39,376 --> 00:02:41,092
They called him Vegas Vic.

36
00:02:41,092 --> 00:02:42,017
His arm waved.

37
00:02:42,017 --> 00:02:43,337
His cigarette moved.

38
00:02:43,337 --> 00:02:47,826
And every fifteen minutes, a speaker boomed out: "Howdy, pardner!"

39
00:02:48,576 --> 00:02:53,102
That same year, the government started testing atomic bombs northwest of town.

40
00:02:53,102 --> 00:03:00,354
One of the most famous photos of the era shows Vegas Vic in the foreground, and a mushroom cloud rising behind the mountains.

41
00:03:01,104 --> 00:03:03,346
Vic kept talking until the late 1960s.

42
00:03:03,346 --> 00:03:11,371
The legend goes that actor Lee Marvin, in town filming a western, complained the cowboy was keeping him up at night, and Vic went quiet.

43
00:03:12,121 --> 00:03:17,124
By the end of the fifties, signs weren't just hanging on buildings anymore.

44
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:24,985
In 1959, YESCO finished the Mint's sign downtown, a sweeping arch of light that rose ninety-six feet over Fremont Street.

45
00:03:24,985 --> 00:03:27,388
The sign had become the architecture.

46
00:03:29,938 --> 00:03:37,388
Meanwhile, a few miles south, a different Las Vegas was growing along Highway 91, the road in from Los Angeles.

47
00:03:38,138 --> 00:03:40,713
Downtown, people walked past the signs.

48
00:03:40,713 --> 00:03:46,258
On the Strip, they drove past them, at fifty miles an hour, from half a mile away.

49
00:03:46,258 --> 00:03:48,305
So the signs had to get bigger.

50
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:49,097
Much bigger.

51
00:03:49,847 --> 00:03:55,729
In 1952, the Sands put its name in flowing neon script on a fifty-six-foot tower.

52
00:03:56,479 --> 00:04:00,698
In 1958, the Stardust opened as the largest hotel in the world.

53
00:04:00,698 --> 00:04:13,889
YESCO designer Kermit Wayne wrapped its entire front in a space-age facade: 217 feet wide, eleven thousand light bulbs, more than a mile of neon, with planets and stars against a painted lunar sky.

54
00:04:13,889 --> 00:04:16,433
The Soviets had just launched Sputnik.

55
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:18,576
America was obsessed with space.

56
00:04:18,576 --> 00:04:22,794
The Stardust sold it back to them, one flashing bulb at a time.

57
00:04:23,544 --> 00:04:31,996
A year later, at the south end of the Strip, a designer named Betty Willis drew a diamond-shaped sign that said Welcome to Fabulous Las Vegas.

58
00:04:31,996 --> 00:04:33,698
And she never copyrighted it.

59
00:04:33,698 --> 00:04:35,282
That one gets its own film.

60
00:04:36,032 --> 00:04:38,328
Then the sign wars really started.

61
00:04:38,328 --> 00:04:43,257
In 1964, the Dunes unveiled a pylon designed by Lee Klay of Federal Sign.

62
00:04:43,257 --> 00:04:47,511
It stood 180 feet tall, the largest electric sign in the world.

63
00:04:47,511 --> 00:04:54,262
It was so tall that maintenance crews rode a small elevator inside one of its legs to reach the top.

64
00:04:55,012 --> 00:05:06,781
The Stardust answered in 1968 with a new roadside tower by Paul Miller of rival sign company Ad-Art: 188 feet of twinkling stars climbing into the desert sky.

65
00:05:07,531 --> 00:05:14,505
Three companies, YESCO, Ad-Art, and Federal, were building the skyline of a city, one casino at a time.

66
00:05:17,055 --> 00:05:25,108
In 1968, architects Robert Venturi, Denise Scott Brown, and Steven Izenour brought a group of Yale students to the Strip.

67
00:05:25,108 --> 00:05:29,368
Serious architects weren't supposed to take Las Vegas seriously.

68
00:05:29,368 --> 00:05:29,967
They did.

69
00:05:30,717 --> 00:05:39,262
Their 1972 book, Learning from Las Vegas, made an argument that rattled the profession: on the Strip, the sign mattered more than the building.

70
00:05:39,262 --> 00:05:46,924
The casinos were plain boxes, what they called "decorated sheds." The real architecture was out by the highway, in steel and neon.

71
00:05:47,674 --> 00:05:52,708
Buildings like the Stardust, a plain structure behind a wall of light, were exactly what they meant.

72
00:05:53,458 --> 00:05:59,562
For the first time, the people who design cities were studying what Las Vegas sign builders had figured out on their own.

73
00:06:02,112 --> 00:06:06,223
Then, in November of 1989, Steve Wynn opened the Mirage.

74
00:06:06,223 --> 00:06:10,409
Out front, instead of a giant pylon, there was a volcano.

75
00:06:10,409 --> 00:06:12,097
It erupted every night.

76
00:06:12,847 --> 00:06:16,695
The message was clear: the building itself was the show.

77
00:06:16,695 --> 00:06:17,588
Pirate ships.

78
00:06:17,588 --> 00:06:18,206
Pyramids.

79
00:06:18,206 --> 00:06:18,825
A castle.

80
00:06:18,825 --> 00:06:20,474
The skyline of New York.

81
00:06:20,474 --> 00:06:25,420
The megaresorts didn't need a 180-foot sign to tell you where they were.

82
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:27,001
You couldn't miss them.

83
00:06:27,751 --> 00:06:30,798
And the old giants started coming down.

84
00:06:30,798 --> 00:06:37,986
The Dunes was imploded in 1993, a televised spectacle that cleared the way for the Bellagio.

85
00:06:37,986 --> 00:06:40,173
The Sands came down in 1996.

86
00:06:40,173 --> 00:06:42,517
The Stardust followed in 2007.

87
00:06:43,267 --> 00:06:47,098
When the Sands closed, there was nowhere to put its massive sign.

88
00:06:47,098 --> 00:06:48,217
So it was scrapped.

89
00:06:48,217 --> 00:06:51,930
That loss pushed the city to get serious about saving its neon.

90
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:54,638
Downtown had its own crisis.

91
00:06:54,638 --> 00:06:57,016
The crowds had moved to the Strip.

92
00:06:57,016 --> 00:07:04,151
So in 1994, the city did something drastic: it closed Fremont Street to cars and built a roof over it.

93
00:07:04,151 --> 00:07:13,383
The Fremont Street Experience opened in December of 1995, a canopy more than a quarter mile long covered in 2.1 million light bulbs.

94
00:07:13,383 --> 00:07:14,432
YESCO built it.

95
00:07:15,182 --> 00:07:20,260
In 2004, the bulbs were replaced with more than twelve million LEDs.

96
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:26,831
In 2019, a new screen went in with forty-nine million, bright enough to see in daylight.

97
00:07:27,582 --> 00:07:28,633
Neon hadn't died.

98
00:07:28,633 --> 00:07:30,673
But it wasn't the future anymore.

99
00:07:33,223 --> 00:07:43,020
For decades, when a Las Vegas sign came down, it often ended up in YESCO's storage yard, rusting in the sun: a graveyard of letters and arrows and stars.

100
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:44,941
Locals called it the boneyard.

101
00:07:45,691 --> 00:07:49,512
In 1996, the Neon Museum was founded to save what was left.

102
00:07:49,512 --> 00:07:55,082
Its first restoration was the Hacienda's horse and rider, a neon cowboy on a palomino.

103
00:07:55,082 --> 00:08:00,457
It was relit that November at the corner of Fremont Street and Las Vegas Boulevard.

104
00:08:01,207 --> 00:08:07,917
In 2012, the museum opened to the public, with its visitor center inside the lobby of the old La Concha Motel.

105
00:08:07,917 --> 00:08:15,359
That seashell-shaped building was designed by architect Paul Revere Williams, cut into pieces, and moved up the Boulevard.

106
00:08:16,109 --> 00:08:19,266
Today, more than 250 signs rest there.

107
00:08:19,266 --> 00:08:21,177
Pieces of the Stardust.

108
00:08:21,177 --> 00:08:22,424
The Desert Inn.

109
00:08:22,424 --> 00:08:23,504
The Flamingo.

110
00:08:26,054 --> 00:08:31,075
On the Fourth of July, 2023, a new sign lit up the Las Vegas skyline.

111
00:08:31,075 --> 00:08:33,767
Its first words were: Hello, World.

112
00:08:34,517 --> 00:08:36,977
The Sphere is 366 feet tall.

113
00:08:36,977 --> 00:08:45,323
Its skin, the Exosphere, is covered in about 1.2 million LED pucks, each packed with 48 diodes.

114
00:08:45,323 --> 00:08:50,595
That's 580,000 square feet: the largest LED screen on Earth.

115
00:08:51,345 --> 00:08:54,335
It's a long way from a neon beer stein on Fremont Street.

116
00:08:55,085 --> 00:08:56,534
But it's the same idea.

117
00:08:56,534 --> 00:09:03,530
In 1931, Tom Young looked at a dusty railroad town and saw a simple problem: how do you get a stranger to stop?

118
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:08,763
Every generation in Las Vegas has solved it differently.

119
00:09:08,763 --> 00:09:09,724
Neon tubing.

120
00:09:09,724 --> 00:09:10,924
Flashing bulbs.

121
00:09:10,924 --> 00:09:12,285
A 180-foot tower.

122
00:09:12,285 --> 00:09:13,086
A volcano.

123
00:09:13,086 --> 00:09:15,487
A building that is a screen.

124
00:09:16,237 --> 00:09:17,922
The answer keeps changing.

125
00:09:17,922 --> 00:09:19,412
The question never has.

126
00:09:20,162 --> 00:09:24,268
Next time: the one sign everybody knows, and the woman who gave it away.

127
00:09:24,268 --> 00:09:26,036
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

